# No backward compatibility

This project is pre-1.0. Do not add or maintain backward-compatibility shims, aliases, or dual code paths.

## Do

- Make breaking changes directly: rename, remove, or reshape APIs in one pass
- Update all call sites, docs, and tests in the same change
- Bump or reset versions/migrations when schemas or contracts change
- Delete dead code instead of leaving deprecated wrappers

## Do not

- Keep old function names, env vars, or routes “for compatibility”
- Add `@deprecated` re-exports that linger indefinitely
- Support multiple response/request shapes for the same endpoint
- Branch on “legacy” flags or version checks to avoid updating consumers

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — compatibility alias
export const getUser = fetchUser;
export { fetchUser as getUser };

// ✅ GOOD — rename and fix all usages
export async function fetchUser(id: string) { ... }
```

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — dual schema support
if (body.legacyField) { ... } else if (body.newField) { ... }

// ✅ GOOD — one shape; migrate data or callers
if (!body.newField) throw new ValidationError("newField is required");
```

When a change breaks external users, document it in release notes or migration guides — do not silently support the old behavior in code.
